1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:08,282
This is a distributed banking system built with gRPC and Protocol Buffers: an account service on port 50051,

2
00:00:08,282 --> 00:00:14,606
a transaction service on 50052 that calls it, a client, and a test suite.

3
00:00:14,606 --> 00:00:17,768
This first recording covers the proto contract.

4
00:00:18,068 --> 00:00:19,834
This is the contract.

5
00:00:19,834 --> 00:00:26,015
gRPC generates both the client stub and the server base class from this file,

6
00:00:26,015 --> 00:00:30,430
so the two sides cannot disagree about the wire format.

7
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:33,079
Proto 3 is the current version,

8
00:00:33,079 --> 00:00:40,585
and the package becomes the namespace: banking.AccountService is the name the server registers and the client resolves.

9
00:00:40,885 --> 00:00:43,985
The assignment asks for getBalance and updateBalance.

10
00:00:43,985 --> 00:00:47,970
I kept those names but capitalised them as PascalCase,

11
00:00:47,970 --> 00:00:53,285
which the Protobuf style guide requires and the generated Python stubs follow.

12
00:00:53,285 --> 00:00:55,056
GetBalance is a read.

13
00:00:55,056 --> 00:00:59,042
UpdateBalance takes a signed amount: positive credits, negative debits,

14
00:00:59,042 --> 00:01:02,585
and a reason field for the audit log.

15
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:08,208
Every message here is a request and response pair for one method.

16
00:01:08,208 --> 00:01:13,531
The numbers after each field, equals one, equals two, are not ordering.

17
00:01:13,531 --> 00:01:20,184
They are the field's identity on the wire and must never be reused once deployed,

18
00:01:20,184 --> 00:01:24,177
because an old client would decode the wrong bytes.

19
00:01:24,477 --> 00:01:27,217
The delta is the interesting field.

20
00:01:27,217 --> 00:01:29,957
Minus fifty debits, plus fifty credits,

21
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:40,462
and the reason travels with it so the server log can say whether money moved because of a transfer or a manual adjustment.

22
00:01:40,762 --> 00:01:44,440
The second service orchestrates transfers and owns history.

23
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:49,956
InitiateTransfer answers with a transaction id, a status, and both new balances,

24
00:01:49,956 --> 00:01:55,932
so the caller does not need a follow-up read to know what happened.

25
00:01:56,232 --> 00:01:58,972
Notice what is missing: no balances.

26
00:01:58,972 --> 00:02:02,169
Money is not passed around in messages.

27
00:02:02,169 --> 00:02:06,280
The transaction service asks the account service for balances,

28
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:09,934
and only the account service ever writes them.

29
00:02:09,934 --> 00:02:12,217
That separation is the design.

30
00:02:12,517 --> 00:02:17,982
A history row stores direction in or out, the counterparty, the amount,

31
00:02:17,982 --> 00:02:19,182
and balance_after.

32
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:29,200
That last field is the useful one: it lets a statement be reconstructed without replaying every transaction from the beginning of time.

33
00:02:29,211 --> 00:02:35,421
History comes back as a repeated field, which is a list on the wire,

34
00:02:35,421 --> 00:02:41,188
plus a count so a client can page without counting the list itself.

35
00:02:41,188 --> 00:02:46,954
The request takes a limit where zero means the server default of ten,

36
00:02:46,954 --> 00:02:50,503
and the server caps it at a hundred.

37
00:02:50,803 --> 00:02:53,660
One protoc run produces two files.

38
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:57,946
banking_pb2 holds the messages, banking_pb2_grpc holds the service stubs.

39
00:02:57,946 --> 00:03:02,707
They are generated, so I never edit them by hand.

40
00:03:03,007 --> 00:03:07,028
Both are committed so a clone runs immediately,

41
00:03:07,028 --> 00:03:12,054
and the README still shows the command that regenerates them.

